p.src:
If the transformation is a symmetry, $\delta L=0$. Since $\epsilon$ is an arbitrary constant factor, $\boxed{
  \frac{d\mathcal Q}{dt}=0,
  \qquad
  \mathcal Q=\sum_i p_i f_i(q)}
  .$ This is the classical-mechanics core of Noether’s theorem for exact, time-independent point symmetries.

